.TH DANBY-RABI 1
.SH NAME
danby-rabi \- a two-level atom driven by a field, as a Bloch vector
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danby/rabi
.SH MODEL
Two levels and the amplitude in each \(em no wave in space at all, which
is what makes this the other half of quantum mechanics from
.IR danby-schrodinger (1).
A qubit is this.  So is a spin in a magnetic field, and so is an atom
driven by a laser; the equations do not know which, which is the point
of writing them in the Bloch form.
.PP
The state is the Bloch vector R = (u, v, w).
.I w
is the population INVERSION \(em the excited fraction less the ground
fraction, so \-1 is an atom certainly in the ground state and +1 one
certainly excited \(em and
.I u
and
.I v
are the coherence, the part with no classical meaning at all.  The
motion is a rotation and nothing else:
.PP
.EX
dR/dt = W x R,   W = (rabi, 0, detuning)
.EE
.PP
which is why the length of R never changes.  A rotation cannot change
it.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Danby/rabi
is a
.IR danbylab (2)
script and nothing else \(em the whole model is the text of
.BR /appl/danby/rabi.spec ,
and editing it needs no compiler.
.SS What to look for
On resonance \(em
.B detuning
zero \(em the vector turns steadily in the plane the diagram draws, and
the atom cycles ground to excited and back for ever.  The excited
fraction is sin squared of
.IR "rabi t" /2
and it reaches exactly 1: the atom is certainly excited, momentarily,
once per turn.  That is Rabi flopping, and it is the thing a
.B pi
pulse in a real experiment is timed to.
.PP
Wind
.B detuning
up and the rotation axis tilts out of the plane.  The vector now sweeps
a cone that does not reach the pole, and the atom can NEVER be fully
excited however long the field is left on.  The ceiling is
.PP
.EX
rabi^2 / (rabi^2 + detuning^2)
.EE
.PP
so at a detuning equal to the Rabi frequency it can only ever get half
way.  Watching the top of the excited curve come down as the detuning
goes up is the experiment, and it is why an off-resonant laser cannot
excite an atom no matter how long you wait.
.SS The diagram
The unit circle the Bloch vector turns in, and the vector itself.  Only
the tip is a marker; the circle is a drawn curve and not a mechanism.
.PP
The circle is the
.IR v - w
plane, which is the plane the vector stays in ON RESONANCE.  With a
detuning it leaves that plane \(em
.I u
becomes non-zero \(em and what is drawn is its projection, so the tip
comes inside the circle rather than off it.  The vector has not
shortened; it is leaning out of the page.
.SS The parameters
.TP
.B rabi
How hard the field drives, in the same units as the clock.  Zero leaves
the atom alone.
.TP
.B detuning
How far the field is from resonance.  Signed: either side is equivalent,
which is worth seeing.
.TP
.B start_inversion
Where
.I w
starts.  \-1 is the ground state.  Anything between is a mixed state,
and its Bloch vector is shorter than 1 and stays shorter \(em the
rotation preserves whatever length it is given.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/danby/rabi.spec
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danby (1),
.IR danby-schrodinger (1),
.IR danbylab (2),
.IR danbylabtest (1)
.SH BUGS
There is no damping.  A real excited atom decays, and with spontaneous
emission in the equations the Bloch vector spirals inward to a steady
state instead of turning for ever \(em which is the more honest picture
of an atom in a laser beam, and is not this model.  The exact
length-conservation that
.IR danbylabtest (1)
checks is a property of the undamped equations and would have to go.
.PP
The rotating-wave approximation is assumed and not shown.  The fast
counter-rotating term has already been dropped, so nothing here wiggles
at the optical frequency, and the Bloch-Siegert shift that term causes
cannot appear.
.PP
The test checks the rotation invariant, complete inversion on resonance,
and the detuned ceiling with a control that fails if the atom is not
actually being driven.  It does not check the diagram at all.
